<DocsLayout title="Welcome & Onboarding" toc={toc}>

  <h1>Welcome & Onboarding</h1>

  <p>
    Welcome to the <strong>Artificial Intelligence and Robotics Lab (AIRLab)</strong> at
    Politecnico di Milano. This guide explains how to access <strong>polimi-cluster</strong>,
    the AIRLab high-performance computing (HPC) cluster, how to store your data, and how to run
    jobs with Slurm. Read it carefully before touching any shared resource.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Before You Start ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="checklist">Before You Start</h2>

  <p>
    Before you can connect to anything, make sure you have obtained the following:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Item</th><th>How to get it</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Building 7 access</td>
        <td>Register as an AIRLab user at <a href="https://airlab.deib.polimi.it/life-in-airlab/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Life@AIRLab</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>VPN access</td>
        <td>Ask your co-supervisor to send a VPN access request to the department ICT. Then, follow this guide to set up <a href="https://www.ict.polimi.it/network/vpn/?lang=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GlobalProtect</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Cluster account (username + temporary password)</td>
        <td>Ask your co-supervisor to request a cluster account for you</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>AIRLab Discord server invite</td>
        <td>Ask your co-supervisor for a single-use invite link. Cluster support happens on Discord (see <a href="#help">Getting Help</a>)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <!-- ── The Cluster ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="cluster">The Cluster</h2>

  <p>
    polimi-cluster is a group of compute servers managed by <strong>Slurm</strong>, a job scheduler.
    You never work on a compute server directly. Instead, you log in to a single machine, the
    <strong>login node</strong>, and ask Slurm to allocate CPUs, memory, and GPUs for your job.
    Slurm then runs the job on a compute node that has those resources free.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Compute node</th><th>CPUs</th><th>GPUs</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>akira</strong></td><td>256</td><td>6 × NVIDIA RTX A6000 (48 GB)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>magrathea</strong></td><td>112</td><td>8 × NVIDIA Quadro RTX 6000 (24 GB)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>arrakis</strong></td><td>192</td><td>6 × NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell (96 GB)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>caladan</strong></td><td>192</td><td>8 × NVIDIA L4 (24 GB)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    CPU counts are logical CPUs. The login node, <code>matteucci-vm-1</code>, also hosts the Slurm
    controller, accounting, and monitoring services. It is <strong>not</strong> a compute node.
    Run <code>sinfo</code> to see the current state of nodes and partitions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use the login node <strong>only</strong> to:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>edit code and Slurm scripts;</li>
    <li>transfer and organise files;</li>
    <li>submit, cancel, and monitor jobs.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-danger">
    <svg class="w-5 h-5 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-red-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
      <line x1="15" y1="9" x2="9" y2="15"/><line x1="9" y1="9" x2="15" y2="15"/>
    </svg>
    <div class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <strong>No computation on the login node.</strong> Do not run training, data preprocessing,
      simulations, notebooks, or CPU-intensive installations on <code>matteucci-vm-1</code>.
      Everyone shares it. Submit these workloads to Slurm, as described in
      <a href={`${base}docs/slurm`}>Running Jobs</a>. Do not SSH into a compute node to start work
      either: Slurm decides where your job runs.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── First Login ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="access">First Login</h2>

  <p>
    Connect to the login node with the username provided to you. When you are connected through
    the VPN, internal DNS may not resolve hostnames, so the IP address is the most reliable option:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="local machine"
    code={`ssh USERNAME@10.75.4.86

# Or, when the hostname resolves:
ssh USERNAME@matteucci-vm-1.inside.dei.polimi.it`}
  />

  <p>
    On your first login, change the temporary password:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="polimi-cluster"
    code={`passwd
# Enter the current (temporary) password, then your new password twice`}
  />

  <p>
    Never share your password or your account with another person. After your first login,
    set up an <strong>SSH key</strong> and a host alias so you can connect with a single short command.
    The <a href={`${base}docs/shell#ssh-keys`}>Shell &amp; Remote Access</a> guide covers both.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Files & Storage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="storage">Files & Storage</h2>

  <p>
    Your home directory is on <strong>megaverse</strong>, the lab's persistent shared network
    storage, and is mounted on the login node and on every compute node. A file you save on the
    login node is therefore immediately visible to your jobs.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="text"
    filename="your home directory"
    code={`~  =  /megaverse/storage/USERNAME      ← code, environments, inputs, results
├── dataset/
│   ├── shared/                        ← datasets shared across the lab
│   └── private/                       ← your own datasets
└── ...`}
  />

  <p>
    Keep important data in your home or dataset directories, not in node-local temporary
    locations. Check how much space you are using with:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="bash"
    filename="polimi-cluster"
    code={`du -sh ~      # size of your home directory
df -h ~       # free space on the underlying storage`}
  />

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <svg class="w-5 h-5 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-accent-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/>
      <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/>
    </svg>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Avoid millions of tiny files.</strong> Network storage handles a few large files far better
      than many small ones. Prefer archives or dataset formats (e.g., <code>tar</code>, HDF5, LMDB,
      WebDataset shards) over a directory containing one file per sample.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Core Responsibilities ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="responsibilities">Core Responsibilities</h2>

  <p>
    Every AIRLab member is expected to uphold the following practices from day one.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Responsibility</th><th>What it means in practice</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Run all computation through Slurm</strong></td>
        <td>Training, preprocessing, evaluation, notebooks, and package installation run as Slurm jobs, never on the login node. See <a href={`${base}docs/slurm`}>Running Jobs</a>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Request only what you need</strong></td>
        <td>Ask Slurm for the CPUs, memory, GPUs, and time your job actually uses. Over-requesting makes your job wait longer and blocks resources for everyone else.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Keep environments reproducible</strong></td>
        <td>Use Conda environments and keep an exported <code>environment.yml</code> in your repository. See <a href={`${base}docs/environments`}>Software &amp; Conda</a>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Version your code on Git</strong></td>
        <td>All project code must live in a private GitHub repository with your co-supervisors added as members.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Document your work</strong></td>
        <td>Every project must have a <code>README.md</code> that a stranger could use to reproduce your results from scratch.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-warning">
    <svg class="w-5 h-5 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-yellow-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
      <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/><line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
    </svg>
    <div class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      If you are ever unsure whether something is allowed, ask your co-supervisor or ask on Discord.
      The shared infrastructure depends on mutual respect.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Getting Help ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="help">Getting Help</h2>

  <p>
    For account, login, storage, Slurm, GPU, Conda, or job issues, ask in the
    <strong>👨🏻‍🏭servers-administration</strong> channel on the AIRLab Discord server. Include the following
    so the issue can be diagnosed quickly:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>your username;</li>
    <li>the job ID;</li>
    <li>the Slurm script or command you ran;</li>
    <li>the relevant output and error log;</li>
    <li>what you expected, and what happened instead.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-danger">
    <svg class="w-5 h-5 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-red-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
         stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
      <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
      <line x1="15" y1="9" x2="9" y2="15"/><line x1="9" y1="9" x2="15" y2="15"/>
    </svg>
    <div class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      Never post passwords, private SSH keys, tokens, or confidential data on Discord.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Ready Checklist ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 id="ready">Before Your First Real Run</h2>

  <p>
    Make sure you can tick every box before you submit your first real experiment:
  </p>

  <ul class="!list-none !ml-0">
    <li>☐ I can log in with <code>ssh USERNAME@10.75.4.86</code>.</li>
    <li>☐ I changed my temporary password.</li>
    <li>☐ I know that <code>~</code> is <code>/megaverse/storage/USERNAME</code>.</li>
    <li>☐ I know where <code>~/dataset/shared</code> and <code>~/dataset/private</code> are.</li>
    <li>☐ I have checked my allowed Slurm QoS assignments (<a href={`${base}docs/slurm#partitions`}>how</a>).</li>
    <li>☐ I have run a short interactive or batch test on the <code>debug</code> partition.</li>
    <li>☐ I request CPUs, RAM, runtime, and GPUs explicitly.</li>
    <li>☐ I use Slurm for all compute-intensive work.</li>
    <li>☐ I know how to use <code>squeue --me</code> and <code>scancel JOBID</code>.</li>
  </ul>

</DocsLayout>
